Subject: Keep a struct as wide as the object C++ builds in it where a field is written narrower than its member

A struct is padded out to the size clang measured, from a tracker told how many
bytes each field takes up. What it is told is how wide the *emitted* field is:
the type codegen writes, which is the type C++ declared for most fields and is
not for a `replaces=` substitution, an array of one, or an instantiation of a
class template. Where that width cannot be worked out the tracker is told the
size clang measured for the member instead, and wherever the emitted field is
narrower than that, the struct is left short of the object C++ builds in it with
nothing to say so: the tracker counts the bytes the target gave the member, pads
the struct out as though the field had covered them, and writes a type narrower
than the run it believed was filled. A caller who asks C++ to construct one in
storage sized from the Rust type gets it built past the end.

An instantiation of a class template is the shape this is about. codegen writes
every instantiation of one template as the same Rust struct - the one bindgen
wrote for the definition, given whichever of the arguments that struct takes -
so the layout clang measured for the instantiation is what the field is only
where that struct happens to be as wide as it. Lay the definition out instead,
with the instantiation's arguments standing in for its parameters, which is what
a member declared with a parameter is written as and the whole of why an
instantiation's width follows its arguments. `struct Holder { Box<std::string>
m; uint32_t n; }` over `template <typename T> struct Box { T value; }` was
sixteen bytes on an LP64 target against C++'s thirty-two, because the string a
`replaces=` substitute stands in for is three pointers and the substitute is
one; laying `Box` out with the substitute as `T` gives eight, and the class is
padded out around it.

Where the definition cannot be laid out, what happens next turns on whether it
is the definition clang instantiated. Two shapes say it is not, and for those
there is no measurement to fall back on: a `replaces=` annotation stands another
template in for this one, so what is written is an instantiation of a different
type from the one clang measured; and a definition which takes none of the
arguments is one Rust struct standing for every instantiation of the template,
which cannot be as wide as more than one of them.
`std::unordered_map<std::string, uint32_t>` is such an instantiation on libc++
and on the Microsoft standard library both - the definition bindgen writes for
it holds one member it kept opaque and comes to a single byte, against the forty
the target gives the instantiation - so a class holding one was a byte wide.
Write the field as a blob of the layout clang measured for those, which is that
layout by construction, whatever the arithmetic could not work out. Everything
else is the definition clang instantiated, whose width follows its arguments,
and is left measured as clang measured the instantiation.

The substitution is read off the definition's own annotation rather than off the
type ref which reaches it, because a type ref is resolved for other reasons too
- a template forward-declared where it is used, say - and a definition reached
that way is the one clang instantiated after all.

A base class is written as a field of the base's own type and takes every route
to a substitution a member takes, so it reaches the same blob by the same walk.
Inside a packed class the blob is asked for at the alignment that class gives
its members, which is never more than the class's own: `blob` asks for
`repr(align(N))` above four-byte alignment, and a packed struct may not
transitively hold such a type at all.

Then the runs of bytes no field covers. A packed class got no padding field
written for one at all, on the grounds that its fields are contiguous - which is
true of a gap worked out from an alignment, and not of a gap the member's own
offset reports. The target really left that one, and the only way a packed class
comes by one is a field written narrower than the member C++ declared:
`#pragma pack(1) struct P { std::string m[3]; int n; };` counted the forty-eight
bytes between the emitted array and `n`, wrote no field for them, and came out
twenty-eight bytes wide against the target's seventy-six. Write that run out, in
bytes, where the class is packed to a single byte - which is where the offset it
is measured from is the offset the emitted struct has. `#pragma pack(N)` for a
wider `N` still aligns each field to the smaller of its own alignment and `N`,
which this tracker does not model, so there the run stays where Rust's own
alignment has always closed it.

And every run of bytes is now written at whatever alignment makes the field the
run: `blob` hands back a whole number of units of the alignment it is asked for,
so a run which is not a whole number of them came back longer than itself and
put the field after it past the offset the target gave it. `pad_struct` already
asked for byte units in that case; `saw_field` did not.

Along with those, `blob` itself: below eight-byte alignment it divided the size
by the alignment and dropped the remainder, so a layout whose size is not a whole
number of its own alignment came back shorter than the layout it was asked for.
Round up instead, which is what a Rust type of that layout comes to and what a
tracker counting one counts. Only an `aligned` attribute reaches such a layout;
anything C++ laid out is already a whole number of its alignment.
